{"provider": "siliconflow", "model": "BAAI/bge-reranker-v2-m3", "model_id": "mdl3076be3c32a0a17f8e9cf5deef89452a9f8886163b0aebd8aa4d1130958e3", "capability": "rerank", "request_id": "9b564ed6-41cc-401e-a642-cae8e7941a09/strategy/sq-1/rerank", "scope": {"owner_id": "p8-real-3de2eca0bea340babcde75ce60c9a366", "knowledge_base_ids": ["96a9eebd-ffb2-4bc6-9bea-3ed015845479"], "document_ids": []}, "required_origin_kbs": [], "body": {"model": "BAAI/bge-reranker-v2-m3", "query": "What man is credited eith inventing both the Leyden jar and the Tribometer?", "documents": ["Summary\nDutch scientist Pieter van Musschenbroek's scientific career and contributions.\n\nPieter van Musschenbroek (1692–1761) was a Dutch scientist who held positions in mathematics, philosophy, medicine, and astronomy across Duisburg, Utrecht, and Leiden. He is credited with inventing the Leyden jar (the first capacitor) in 1746, performed pioneering work on buckling of compressed struts, and was among the first (from 1729) to describe testing machines for tension, compression, and flexure. He also contributed to dynamic plasticity through a 1739 study of impact penetration of butter by a wooden stick.", "Pieter van Musschenbroek (14 March 1692 – 19 September 1761) was a Dutch scientist.\n He was a professor in Duisburg, Utrecht, and Leiden, where he held positions in mathematics, philosophy, medicine, and astronomy.\n He is credited with the invention of the first capacitor in 1746: the Leyden jar.\n He performed pioneering work on the buckling of compressed struts.", "Summary\nA Leyden jar stores static electricity using a glass jar with foil on both surfaces and a terminal through the lid.\n\nA Leyden jar is a historical electrostatic device consisting of a glass jar with metal foil on its inner and outer surfaces, plus a metal terminal through the lid for electrical contact. It was the original form of a capacitor, known as a 'condenser'.", "A Leyden jar, or Leiden jar, is a device that \"stores\" static electricity between two electrodes on the inside and outside of a glass jar.\n A Leyden jar typically consists of a glass jar with metal foil cemented to the inside and the outside surfaces, and a metal terminal projecting vertically through the jar lid to make contact with the inner foil.\n It was the original form of a capacitor (originally known as a \"condenser\").", "A jar was an early unit of capacitance once used by the British Royal Navy.\n The term originated as the capacitance of a Leyden jar.\n Its value is such that one farad is jars and one jar is 1111 picofarads.", "Summary\nAn early British Navy unit of capacitance based on the Leyden jar.\n\nThe jar was an early unit of capacitance used by the British Royal Navy, originally defined as the capacitance of a Leyden jar. One farad equals approximately 999 jars, and one jar equals approximately 1.111 picofarads.", "Summary\nA tribometer is an instrument for measuring friction and wear between two surfaces.\n\nThe document defines a tribometer as an instrument for measuring tribological quantities like coefficient of friction, friction force, and wear volume between two contacting surfaces. It states the instrument was invented by the 18th-century Dutch scientist Musschenbroek.", "Summary\nChristopher Henry Gibbs, a British antiques dealer and collector credited with inventing Swinging London.\n\nChristopher Henry Gibbs (born 29 July 1938) is a British antiques dealer and collector who was influential in 1960s London men's fashion and interior design. He is credited with inventing 'Swinging London' and known as the 'King of Chelsea' and 'London's most famous antiques dealer.' The New York Times described him as a man of infinite taste, judgment and experience who introduced a whole generation to the distressed bohemian style of interior design.", "A tribometer is an instrument that measures tribological quantities, such as coefficient of friction, friction force, and wear volume, between two surfaces in contact.\n It was invented by the 18th century Dutch scientist Musschenbroek", "Summary\nBenjamin Franklin's electrostatic machine for generating high-voltage static electricity.\n\nFranklin's electrostatic machine is a high-voltage static electricity generator invented by Benjamin Franklin in the mid-18th century. Its key components include a spinning glass globe driven by a crank, a cloth pad in contact with the globe, metal needles to conduct away charge, and a Leyden jar to accumulate the charge. Franklin's experiments with this device led to new theories about electricity and the invention of the lightning rod.", "Franklin bells (also known as Gordon’s Bells or lightning bells) are an early demonstration of electric charge designed to work with a Leyden jar.\n Franklin bells are only a qualitative indicator of electric charge and were used for simple demonstrations rather than research.\n This was the first device that converted electrical energy into mechanical energy in the form of continuous mechanical motion, in this case, the moving of a bell clapper back and forth between two oppositely charged bells.", "Christopher Henry Gibbs (born 29 July 1938) is a British antiques dealer and collector who was also an influential figure in men's fashion and interior design in 1960s London.\n He has been credited with inventing Swinging London, and has been called the \"King of Chelsea\" and \"London's most famous antiques dealer\".\n The \"New York Times\" described him as a \"man of infinite taste, judgment and experience, the one who introduced a whole generation to the distressed bohemian style of interior design.\"", "Summary\nRoman freedman credited with inventing the Latin letter G and opening the first recorded private elementary school.\n\nSpurius Carvilius Ruga (fl. 230 BC) was a Roman freedman credited with inventing the Latin letter G, which replaced the confusing dual use of C for both /k/ and /g/ sounds. He also opened the first recorded private elementary school in history. His own name, SPVRIVS CARVILIVS RVCA, illustrates the letter confusion he sought to resolve.", "Summary\nFranklin bells convert electrical energy into continuous mechanical motion via a swinging clapper between two oppositely\n\nFranklin bells are an early electrostatic demonstration device using a Leyden jar, serving as a qualitative indicator of electric charge. They represent the first device to convert electrical energy into continuous mechanical motion, achieved by swinging a bell clapper back and forth between two oppositely charged bells.", "The Old Jar Craftsman (독짓는 늙은이 - \"Dokjinneun neulgeuni\") \"aka\" Old Man Making a Jar is a 1969 South Korean film directed by Choi Ha-won.\n It was awarded Best Film at the Blue Dragon Film Awards ceremony.\n The film was selected as the South Korean entry for the Best Foreign Language Film at the 42nd Academy Awards, but was not accepted as a nominee.", "Spurius Carvilius Ruga (fl.\n 230 BC) was the freedman of Spurius Carvilius Maximus Ruga.\n He is often credited with inventing the Latin letter G.\n His invention would have been quickly adopted in the Roman Republic, because the letter C was, at the time, confusingly used both for the /k/ and /g/ sounds.\n For example, Ruga's own name contained this confusion: \"SPVRIVS CARVILIVS RVCA\" (At that time, \"U\" and \"V\" were also the same letter).", "After getting over his initial shock, the boy starts to take care of him.\n The story follows their relationship over the next few days between John and 'Man', with the Man showing himself to be demanding, bossy and messy, but nevertheless a bond forms between the pair.\n Their time together involves many funny and peculiar moments, such as an odd obsession with Frank Cooper's Oxford marmalade, using socks for jumpers, and a near-death collision with a marmalade jar.", "Franklin's electrostatic machine is a high-voltage static electricity generating device used by Benjamin Franklin in the mid-eighteenth century for research into electrical phenomena.\n Its key components are a glass globe which turned on an axis via a crank; a cloth pad in contact with the spinning globe; a set of metal needles to conduct away the charge developed on the globe by its friction with the pad; and a Leyden jara high-voltage capacitorto accumulate the charge.", "Franklin's experiments with the machine eventually led to new theories about electricity and inventing the lightning rod.", "The Man is a graphic novella for children, written and illustrated by Raymond Briggs and published by Julia MacRae Books in 1992.\n It tells the humorous story of a boy, John, who is visited by the titular Man, a minuscule human (homunculus) who arrives in the boy's bedroom unclothed and hungry.\n After getting over his initial shock, the boy starts to take care of him.", "A tragic hero is the protagonist of a tragedy in drama.\n In his \"Poetics\", Aristotle records the descriptions of the tragic hero to the playwright and strictly defines the place that the tragic hero must play and the kind of man he must be.\n Aristotle based his observations on previous dramas.\n Many of the most famous instances of tragic heroes appear in Greek literature, most notably the works of Sophocles and Euripides.", "Summary\n一部儿童图文小说，讲述男孩John与一个微小人形'Man'的奇妙相处故事。\n\n《The Man》是一部由Raymond Briggs创作的儿童图文小说，讲述男孩John被一个名为'Man'的微小人形（小人）拜访的故事。Man裸体且饥饿地出现在John的卧室，男孩克服震惊后开始照顾他。两人相处几天后，Man展现出要求多、爱指挥且邋遢的特点，但逐渐建立起深厚的情感纽带。故事包含许多幽默奇特的片段，如对Frank Cooper牛津果酱的奇怪执念、用袜子当毛衣、以及差点被果酱罐砸中的惊险经历。", "Ruga was also the first man in recorded history to open a private elementary school.", "He performed pioneering work on the buckling of compressed struts.\n Musschenbroek was also one of the first scientists (1729) to provide detailed descriptions of testing machines for tension, compression, and flexure testing.\n An early example of a problem in dynamic plasticity was described in the 1739 paper (in the form of the penetration of butter by a wooden stick subjected to impact by a wooden sphere).", "A Germanic hero is the protagonist of certain works of early medieval literature mostly in Germanic languages.\n This hero is always a warrior, concerned both with his reputation and fame, and with his political responsibilities.\n The way in which he \"copes with the blows of fate\" is extremely important.\n He may be distinguished from the classical hero in that his adventures are less individualistic, and from the tragic hero because his death is heroic rather than tragic.", "The average height is from seven to ten feet.\n In the south-east angle is a rude temple of Bhairavnath and a few houses with the remains of Man y more.\n On the right side of the southern gate is a well preserved stone pond about thirty yards square with steps leading down to it.\n Next to and on the north of Bhairavnath's temple is another pond.\n The way up to the fort proper or upper and lower citadels is from the north side.", "Leonard Whiting (born 30 June 1950) is an English actor and singer who is best known for his role as Romeo in the 1968 Zeffirelli film version of \"Romeo and Juliet\" opposite Olivia Hussey's Juliet, a role which earned him the Golden Globe Award for New Star of the Year - Actor.\n He was touted as a star in the making, the next Laurence Olivier and the next great British actor.", "Romeo Montague (Italian: \"Romeo Montecchi\" ) is the protagonist of William Shakespeare's tragedy \"Romeo and Juliet\".\n The son of Montague and his wife, he secretly loves and marries Juliet, a member of the rival House of Capulet.\n Forced into exile after slaying Juliet's cousin, Tybalt, in a duel, Romeo commits suicide upon hearing falsely of Juliet's death.", "Summary\nBiographical profile of American poet Jeanne Marie Beaumont covering her career, publications, teaching, and film adapta\n\nJeanne Marie Beaumont is an American poet with four poetry collections, including 'Letters from Limbo' (2016) and 'Burning of the Three Fires' (2010). Her work has appeared in numerous major literary journals, and she has been featured on Garrison Keillor's The Writer's Almanac. In 2006, filmmaker Jay Rosenblatt adapted her poem 'Afraid So' into a film narrated by Keillor, screened at international film festivals and the Museum of Modern Art. Beaumont co-edited 'American Letters & Commentary' (1992–2000), served as a judge for the 2011 Cider Press Review Book Award, and has taught at Rutgers University and the 92nd Street Y, while also serving as Director of The Frost Place Advanced Seminar (2007–2010) and on the faculty of the Stonecoast MFA Program.", "Something Wicked This Way Comes is a 1962 dark fantasy novel by Ray Bradbury.\n It is about 13-year-old best friends, Jim Nightshade and William Halloway, and their nightmarish experience with a traveling carnival that comes to their Midwestern town one October, and how the boys learn about combatting fear.\n The carnival's leader is the mysterious \"Mr. Dark\" who seemingly wields the power to grant the citizenry's secret desires."]}, "headers_saved": false}